    <string name="crystalx_name" translatable="false">CrystalX+</string>
    <string name="crystalx_summary">Fuller tone, crisper detail and a stereo image tuned to your headphones or speaker, with every peak held below clipping.</string>
    <string name="crystalx_about_title">About CrystalX+</string>
    <string name="crystalx_about_body">CrystalX+ refines every track in four stages.\n\n• Tonal balance: gentle shelves modelled on the ear\'s equal-loudness contours add body to the bass and air to the treble, while a dynamic bass control keeps loud passages from booming.\n• Harmonic exciter: a subtle stage, oversampled 2× to stay clean, adds fine upper harmonics for crisper detail.\n• Output-aware stereo: crossfeed on headphones for a natural, less tiring image; added width on the speaker.\n• True-peak limiter: measures peaks between samples as well as on them, and holds every one below clipping.\n\nVery loud, heavily compressed masters play slightly quieter with CrystalX+ on.</string>
    <string name="renovapure_name" translatable="false">Seion RenovaPure</string>
    <string name="renovapure_summary">Detects where lossy compression cut the treble and restores what was lost from the top octave, designed never to sound brighter than the lossless original. Files with their full treble play untouched.</string>
    <string name="renovapure_about_title">About Seion RenovaPure</string>
    <string name="renovapure_about_body">Lossy formats save space by discarding the highest frequencies, which dulls cymbals, breath and the sound of the room.\n\nSeion RenovaPure analyses each track\'s spectrum to find the frequency where the encoder cut the treble, then rebuilds the missing band from the music just below it, following its natural roll-off. The restored band is held slightly below the level the recording would have had, so the result is designed never to sound brighter than the lossless original, and an onset guard keeps attacks from smearing.\n\nWhen no cutoff is found, as on files with their full treble, the music passes through bit for bit.</string>
